1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,512
It is August twelfth, nineteen eighty five.

2
00:00:02,512 --> 00:00:07,804
At Haneda Airport in Tokyo, a famous man is boarding a flight he never meant to take.

3
00:00:08,933 --> 00:00:10,740
His name is Kyu Sakamoto.

4
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:17,606
In nineteen sixty three, he became the first Japanese artist ever to hit number one on the American charts.

5
00:00:18,733 --> 00:00:20,553
He always flew a different airline.

6
00:00:20,553 --> 00:00:24,193
But tonight, every seat on it is booked solid for the holiday.

7
00:00:24,193 --> 00:00:28,137
So his ticket gets moved, at the last minute, onto this one instead.

8
00:00:29,266 --> 00:00:33,707
His wife does not know he switched flights. She will find out from the passenger list.

9
00:00:34,833 --> 00:00:41,383
The aircraft is a Boeing seven forty seven, over a decade old, flown thousands of times before.

10
00:00:41,383 --> 00:00:44,080
Five hundred and nine passengers. Fifteen crew.

11
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:50,688
In the cockpit, the most experienced seven forty seven captain in the airline isn't flying tonight.

12
00:00:50,688 --> 00:00:53,088
He's sitting in the co-pilot's seat, teaching.

13
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:57,355
Masami Takahama has flown this exact route for years.

14
00:00:57,355 --> 00:00:59,437
Twelve thousand four hundred flight hours.

15
00:00:59,437 --> 00:01:02,906
Nearly five thousand of them on this specific aircraft type.

16
00:01:04,033 --> 00:01:09,784
In twelve minutes, this aircraft will suffer the most catastrophic structural failure in aviation history.

17
00:01:09,784 --> 00:01:15,919
And then it will keep flying anyway — completely uncontrollable — for thirty two more minutes.

18
00:01:17,066 --> 00:01:20,505
Five hundred and twenty people will not survive it.

19
00:01:20,505 --> 00:01:26,235
It remains, to this day, the deadliest single aircraft disaster in the history of flight.

20
00:01:27,366 --> 00:01:33,322
The cause traces back seven years, to a repair Boeing was supposed to get right — and quietly didn't.

21
00:01:34,466 --> 00:01:36,425
You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

22
00:01:37,566 --> 00:01:39,839
Japan Airlines 123.

23
00:01:40,966 --> 00:01:45,842
Seven years before this flight, this exact aircraft already tried to kill everyone on board once.

24
00:01:45,842 --> 00:01:48,280
Nobody outside the industry ever heard about it.

25
00:01:49,399 --> 00:01:51,306
June second, nineteen seventy eight.

26
00:01:51,306 --> 00:01:57,027
This exact same aircraft is landing at Osaka on a routine domestic flight from Tokyo.

27
00:01:58,166 --> 00:02:03,764
It bounces on the first touchdown. On the second, the tail slams into the runway.

28
00:02:03,764 --> 00:02:08,615
Twenty five people are hurt. Nobody dies. The story ends there, everyone assumes.

29
00:02:09,733 --> 00:02:20,574
The impact tears open the aircraft's aft pressure bulkhead — a domed wall of metal, built to hold the entire cabin's air pressure in, every single flight, for the rest of the aircraft's life.

30
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:25,163
JAL patches it temporarily, then flies the aircraft to Tokyo.

31
00:02:25,163 --> 00:02:29,667
There, Boeing sends its own repair crew to fix it properly, for good.

32
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:32,790
Boeing's repair instructions are exact.

33
00:02:32,790 --> 00:02:40,753
The torn section of bulkhead needs one new splice plate, correctly sized, fastened down with two full rows of rivets.

34
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:43,152
That is not what happens.

35
00:02:43,152 --> 00:02:50,415
The crew on the ground installs a plate that's too narrow for the job — and packs the gap with a separate filler piece instead of the real part.

36
00:02:51,533 --> 00:02:58,923
According to Japan's own investigators, the result is that a joint meant to be held by two rows of rivets ends up held by one.

37
00:02:58,923 --> 00:03:02,766
Its strength: cut to about seventy percent of what the design called for.

38
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:06,404
No written record of the substitution is ever filed.

39
00:03:06,404 --> 00:03:11,135
A Boeing inspector checks the work — and does not catch that it doesn't match the drawing.

40
00:03:12,266 --> 00:03:14,356
Sealant is later applied along the repair's edge.

41
00:03:14,356 --> 00:03:18,535
From that point on, nobody can even see the joint well enough to check it again.

42
00:03:19,666 --> 00:03:24,081
July twelfth, nineteen seventy eight. The aircraft passes its post-repair inspection.

43
00:03:24,081 --> 00:03:28,495
It is cleared to fly. Nobody involved knows anything is wrong.

44
00:03:29,633 --> 00:03:35,887
Every single flight after that day pressurizes the cabin, then depressurizes it again on descent.

45
00:03:35,887 --> 00:03:40,474
Like inflating a balloon. Seven thousand, four hundred times a year.

46
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:47,181
At the weakened rivet holes, microscopic cracks begin forming almost immediately after the repair.

47
00:03:47,181 --> 00:03:50,769
Too small to see. Growing with every single flight.

48
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:56,239
The aircraft keeps flying, completely normally, for seven more years.

49
00:03:56,239 --> 00:03:59,711
Twelve thousand, three hundred and nineteen more flights.

50
00:04:00,833 --> 00:04:02,231
December nineteen eighty four.

51
00:04:02,231 --> 00:04:07,476
Mechanics perform a scheduled inspection — the kind every aircraft gets every few thousand hours.

52
00:04:07,476 --> 00:04:09,924
They check the area. They find nothing.

53
00:04:11,066 --> 00:04:12,526
Investigators later calculate why.

54
00:04:12,526 --> 00:04:23,108
By that inspection, the cracks were already about a centimeter long — but so thin, a visual check had only around a one-in-ten chance of catching any single one.

55
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:33,324
This is what one of those cracks actually looked like, magnified thousands of times under an electron microscope — every faint ridge, a single flight cycle.

56
00:04:34,466 --> 00:04:35,859
This was not simple negligence.

57
00:04:35,859 --> 00:04:41,153
It was a defect built to hide from the exact kind of inspection anyone would have thought to run.

58
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:52,174
By August of nineteen eighty five, the cracks have grown and linked together into a single fracture line, almost the length of two strides, hidden inside the tail of the aircraft.

59
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:55,378
Nobody on the ground knows it's there.

60
00:04:55,378 --> 00:04:58,942
Nobody boarding tonight's flight has any way of knowing it's there either.

61
00:05:00,066 --> 00:05:01,176
Six eighteen PM.

62
00:05:01,176 --> 00:05:07,094
Japan Airlines Flight one twenty three pushes back from the gate at Haneda, bound for Osaka.

63
00:05:07,094 --> 00:05:09,313
A routine, forty-five minute domestic hop.

64
00:05:10,433 --> 00:05:15,245
Twelve minutes later, climbing toward twenty four thousand feet, there is a loud bang.

65
00:05:15,245 --> 00:05:19,027
Both pilots say the same three words, at the same instant.

66
00:05:20,166 --> 00:05:22,581
That bang is the aft pressure bulkhead.

67
00:05:22,581 --> 00:05:28,447
Seven years of hidden cracks finally give way, all at once, in a fraction of a second.

68
00:05:29,566 --> 00:05:39,597
Cabin air blasts backward into the unpressurized tail section, tearing through it, blowing out the base of the vertical fin, and severing every hydraulic line that controls the aircraft.

69
00:05:40,733 --> 00:05:45,907
According to investigators, this entire chain of destruction takes place in a matter of seconds.

70
00:05:45,907 --> 00:05:52,462
A modern airliner has three, sometimes four separate hydraulic systems specifically so one failure can never take them all.

71
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:56,386
This failure takes all of them at once.

72
00:05:56,386 --> 00:05:59,869
In ninety seconds, the flight engineer confirms it out loud.

73
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,829
A Boeing seven forty seven has no manual backup for its flight controls.

74
00:06:05,829 --> 00:06:11,031
Without hydraulics, the rudder, the elevators, the ailerons — none of them respond anymore.

75
00:06:12,166 --> 00:06:17,573
The only thing the crew has left to fly the airplane with, for the rest of this flight, is the throttles.

76
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:24,107
Four minutes after the bang, the aircraft radios Tokyo Control with the words that will define the next half hour.

77
00:06:25,233 --> 00:06:30,297
It will remain the longest sustained inflight emergency in the history of commercial aviation.

78
00:06:30,297 --> 00:06:34,637
Thirty two minutes of an aircraft that will not obey its pilots.

79
00:06:35,766 --> 00:06:40,572
The aircraft pitches and rolls on its own, in long, slow, sickening oscillations.

80
00:06:40,572 --> 00:06:47,965
The only way the crew can fight it at all is by adding and cutting engine power, again and again.

81
00:06:49,100 --> 00:06:57,492
Investigators later concluded that true directional steering by engine power alone was almost impossible on this aircraft — and found no evidence the crew ever managed it.

82
00:06:57,492 --> 00:07:01,534
They weren't steering. They were just trying to keep it in the air.

83
00:07:02,666 --> 00:07:06,257
The captain requests an emergency return to Haneda.

84
00:07:06,257 --> 00:07:12,540
Instead, the crippled aircraft drifts west, out over Suruga Bay, completely outside anyone's control.

85
00:07:13,666 --> 00:07:24,115
Near Mount Fuji, the crew lowers the landing gear by hand, using a backup mechanical release — the first control input beyond raw engine power that actually does something.

86
00:07:25,233 --> 00:07:35,264
Twenty three minutes in, still fighting for altitude, the aircraft swings low over the mountains west of Tokyo — and for one moment, everyone in the cockpit can see exactly what's ahead.

87
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:37,797
Hey. Mountain.

88
00:07:38,933 --> 00:07:40,417
Yes, please.

89
00:07:41,566 --> 00:07:45,275
Mountain — take control, right. Right turn.

90
00:07:46,400 --> 00:07:47,465
We'll hit a mountain!

91
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:49,524
Max power.

92
00:07:50,666 --> 00:07:51,906
They clear it. Barely.

93
00:07:51,906 --> 00:07:59,652
It is a near miss — nine minutes before the actual crash, and roughly nine minutes before anyone on board runs out of time completely.

94
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:06,207
In the cabin, passengers who understand what's happening start doing the only thing left to do.

95
00:08:07,333 --> 00:08:13,071
A man named Hirotsugu Kawaguchi writes to his family: it has been five minutes now, he writes.

96
00:08:13,071 --> 00:08:15,771
I don't want to take any more planes.

97
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:21,281
Kazuo Yoshimura writes a note asking his wife to look after their children.

98
00:08:21,281 --> 00:08:27,349
Several other passengers write their own — messages of love, fear, instructions for the people they're leaving behind.

99
00:08:28,466 --> 00:08:38,497
One of the flight attendants, Yumiko Tsushima, spends part of those final minutes writing out safety instructions for the passengers around her — in Japanese, then in English.

100
00:08:39,633 --> 00:08:42,141
Several of those notes survive the crash.

101
00:08:42,141 --> 00:08:49,664
Today, translated, they sit behind glass at Japan Airlines' own safety museum — read by every new employee the airline hires.

102
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:52,290
Twenty eight minutes in.

103
00:08:52,290 --> 00:08:57,879
The aircraft climbs, briefly, then starts descending again, still rolling and pitching on its own.

104
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:03,231
Six minutes after that, the aircraft reports its position one last time.

105
00:09:03,231 --> 00:09:06,758
Approach control offers two nearby airports. There is no response.

106
00:09:06,758 --> 00:09:08,874
There will be no more responses.

107
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,500
Raise the nose. Raise the nose.

108
00:09:13,633 --> 00:09:15,102
Power. Power.

109
00:09:16,233 --> 00:09:19,942
Then the ground proximity warning system takes over the cockpit entirely.

110
00:09:21,066 --> 00:09:25,820
Six times, the same automated voice repeats the only warning it knows how to give.

111
00:09:26,966 --> 00:09:30,388
The official cockpit voice recording ends eighteen seconds later.

112
00:09:30,388 --> 00:09:33,810
Thirty two minutes and sixteen seconds after it began.

113
00:09:34,933 --> 00:09:44,494
The aircraft clips a ridge, then a single tree, then comes down nose-first onto a mountainside in Gunma Prefecture, on a slope that will later be named Osutaka Ridge.

114
00:09:45,633 --> 00:09:49,760
Five hundred and twenty four people were on board Japan Airlines Flight one twenty three.

115
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:57,248
It takes rescuers almost sixteen hours to reach the crash site — through mountainous terrain, in total darkness.

116
00:09:58,366 --> 00:10:04,893
Japan's own official investigation later calls the delay, quote, justifiable, given the terrain and the darkness.

117
00:10:04,893 --> 00:10:08,972
Survivors and journalists at the time told a harsher story.

118
00:10:10,100 --> 00:10:19,426
One of the four people who survived the crash later described hearing cries for help around her in the wreckage — cries that, through the long night, one by one, went quiet.

119
00:10:20,566 --> 00:10:27,358
Doctors who later treated the survivors said publicly that finding them even ten hours sooner might have saved more lives.

120
00:10:27,358 --> 00:10:29,735
Whether that's true has never been settled.

121
00:10:30,866 --> 00:10:36,112
At ten forty five the next morning, rescuers finally find survivors. All four are women.

122
00:10:36,112 --> 00:10:41,707
All four were sitting within the same few rows, near the very back of the aircraft.

123
00:10:42,833 --> 00:10:49,134
One of them, an off-duty flight attendant named Yumi Ochiai, had survived sixteen hours alone in the wreckage.

124
00:10:49,134 --> 00:10:51,584
Within eight months, she was flying again.

125
00:10:52,733 --> 00:10:55,261
Another survivor was an eight year old girl.

126
00:10:55,261 --> 00:11:02,529
Her mother, badly injured beside her, later said her daughter kept talking to her all night — kept her awake, kept her alive.

127
00:11:03,666 --> 00:11:08,371
Investigators worked the wreckage for two months, and the case for nearly two years.

128
00:11:08,371 --> 00:11:11,059
Their final report runs over three hundred pages.

129
00:11:12,200 --> 00:11:19,593
This is what they actually recovered of the aft pressure bulkhead — the exact part that failed, laid out and photographed for the record.

130
00:11:20,733 --> 00:11:24,129
Their conclusion, verbatim, from the official report.

131
00:11:25,266 --> 00:11:34,435
The initiation and propagation of the fatigue cracks, they wrote, are attributable to the improper repairs of the bulkhead conducted in nineteen seventy eight.

132
00:11:35,566 --> 00:11:41,885
Three weeks after the crash, Boeing publicly acknowledged that its repair crew had not followed its own specification.

133
00:11:41,885 --> 00:11:46,799
It stopped short, at the time, of directly confirming the repair caused the accident.

134
00:11:47,933 --> 00:11:54,558
Japanese prosecutors referred twenty people for possible criminal negligence charges — including four Boeing employees.

135
00:11:54,558 --> 00:11:57,650
In the end, nobody was ever indicted.

136
00:11:58,800 --> 00:12:00,765
But the aircraft itself changed.

137
00:12:00,765 --> 00:12:14,526
Within a year, Japan's aviation authority ordered every Boeing seven forty seven with high flight cycles fully re-inspected, and a structural cover installed over the exact opening that let pressurized air destroy this aircraft's tail.

138
00:12:15,666 --> 00:12:24,835
A second directive followed months later, permanently folding this exact failure mode into how every aging seven forty seven gets inspected, worldwide, from then on.

139
00:12:25,966 --> 00:12:34,858
Two men connected to the repair and its inspection — a JAL maintenance manager and the engineer who had cleared the aircraft as airworthy — later died by suicide.

140
00:12:34,858 --> 00:12:37,617
Both deaths were widely reported as driven by guilt.

141
00:12:38,766 --> 00:12:41,822
The airline's president resigned twelve days after the crash.

142
00:12:41,822 --> 00:12:46,237
He is reported to have personally visited grieving families before he stepped down.

143
00:12:47,366 --> 00:12:54,445
Kyu Sakamoto — the singer who took the last available seat — died on that mountainside along with almost everyone else aboard.

144
00:12:55,566 --> 00:12:59,746
One passenger, a banker named Akihisa Yukawa, also died that night.

145
00:12:59,746 --> 00:13:03,925
His daughter was born five weeks later. She never met him.

146
00:13:05,066 --> 00:13:07,806
She grew up to become a professional violinist.

147
00:13:07,806 --> 00:13:14,313
In interviews, she has spoken openly about a father she only ever knew through photographs, and through this story.

148
00:13:15,433 --> 00:13:18,407
The crash site itself is now called Osutaka Ridge.

149
00:13:18,407 --> 00:13:22,042
A stone monument there lists all five hundred and twenty names.

150
00:13:23,166 --> 00:13:29,644
Every August twelfth, bereaved families and volunteers still climb the mountain trail to Osutaka Ridge to pray at the site.

151
00:13:29,644 --> 00:13:32,883
They did it again this year, on the fortieth anniversary.

152
00:13:34,033 --> 00:13:42,120
At Haneda Airport today, Japan Airlines keeps pieces of the actual wreckage on permanent display — not for visitors, but for its own employees.

153
00:13:42,120 --> 00:13:44,142
Every new hire walks through it.

154
00:13:45,266 --> 00:13:47,963
There was no single villain in this story.

155
00:13:47,963 --> 00:13:55,715
There was a hard landing, a rushed repair, one wrong part, and seven years in which nobody had a reason to look again.

156
00:13:56,833 --> 00:14:03,755
Somewhere, right now, in the tail of an aircraft you'll never see, is a repair nobody has ever had a reason to double check.

157
00:14:04,900 --> 00:14:07,811
That isn't a reason to be afraid of flying.

158
00:14:07,811 --> 00:14:15,898
It's the reason aviation keeps rewriting its own rulebook, one investigation at a time — including the one that came out of this exact mountainside.

159
00:14:17,033 --> 00:14:31,479
If a botched repair, buried for seven years, made you angry, wait until you see how Boeing killed three hundred and forty six people the exact same way, decades later — that's our episode on the seven three seven MAX, right here on this channel.

160
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:36,373
Wait until you see our seven three seven MAX episode next.

161
00:14:36,373 --> 00:14:40,489
Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown, for the investigations everyone else got wrong.

